  <!-- Article Body -->
  <section class="bg-[#F8F8F5] py-16">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-gray-200 shadow-sm p-8 md:p-12 prose prose-lg max-w-none">

        <p class="text-xl text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          Your deadbolt sticks, or the key turns hard, or the door just won't latch. Now you're trying to decide whether to repair or replace a door lock in Dover, DE, and every answer online says "it depends." A plainer rule works better. Repair a worn part or an alignment problem. Replace a lock that's cracked, corroded, or keeps failing after a fix.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Before you decide, check one thing. Some "broken locks" are really door problems: the lock is fine, but the door has shifted and the bolt can't line up with the frame. Below you'll find a quick test, a symptom table, and a note on when rekeying beats both.
        </p>

        <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-[#A61E22] bg-[#F8F8F5] px-6 py-4 my-8 not-italic">
          <p class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mb-2">Key Takeaways</p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-gray-700 space-y-1 mb-0">
            <li>If a lock sticks only when the door is closed, suspect door alignment, not the lock.</li>
            <li>Try graphite, snug hinge screws, and a strike plate adjustment first.</li>
            <li>Repair one worn part on a sound lock. Replace a lock that's cracked, rusted, or failing again.</li>
            <li>Rekey when the real issue is who has keys.</li>
          </ul>
        </blockquote>

        <nav aria-label="Table of contents" class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6 mb-10 not-prose">
          <p class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mb-3">In This Article</p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-[#A61E22] space-y-1 text-sm">
            <li><a href="#is-it-the-lock-or-the-door-the-door-open-test" class="hover:underline">Is It the Lock or the Door? The Door-Open Test</a></li>
            <li><a href="#quick-fixes-to-try-first" class="hover:underline">Quick Fixes to Try First</a></li>
            <li><a href="#when-repair-is-the-right-call" class="hover:underline">When Repair Is the Right Call</a></li>
            <li><a href="#when-replacement-is-the-right-call" class="hover:underline">When Replacement Is the Right Call</a></li>
            <li><a href="#repair-vs-replace-which-fits-your-problem" class="hover:underline">Repair vs. Replace: Which Fits Your Problem?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#should-you-rekey-instead" class="hover:underline">Should You Rekey Instead?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#when-should-you-call-a-locksmith-in-dover" class="hover:underline">When Should You Call a Locksmith in Dover?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#the-short-answer" class="hover:underline">The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq" class="hover:underline">Frequently Asked Questions</a></li>
          </ul>
        </nav>

        <h2 id="is-it-the-lock-or-the-door-the-door-open-test" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Is It the Lock or the Door? The Door-Open Test</h2>

        <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-[#A61E22] bg-[#F8F8F5] px-6 py-4 my-8 not-italic">
          <p class="text-gray-700 mb-0"><strong class="text-[#1A1F22]">In short:</strong> The Door-Open Test separates a failing lock from a misaligned door. Work the deadbolt with the door open, then closed. If it moves freely open but binds shut, the problem is usually alignment between the bolt and strike plate, not the lock. <a href="https://www.hunker.com/12315781/how-to-fix-a-jammed-door-lock/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hunker</a> names that misalignment as a cause of sticking.</p>
        </blockquote>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          You can tell in about two minutes, and the steps below show how.
        </p>

        <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-[#A61E22] bg-[#F8F8F5] px-6 py-4 my-8 not-italic">
          <p class="text-gray-700 mb-0"><strong class="text-[#1A1F22]">What is the Door-Open Test?</strong> The Door-Open Test is a three-step check that separates a failing lock from a misaligned door. You work the bolt with the door open, then closed, then look at where the bolt meets the strike plate. Whether the trouble shows up with the door open or only closed tells you where to look.</p>
        </blockquote>

        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Open the door and work the lock.</strong> Turn the thumb turn from inside, then lock and unlock with the key from outside. Stiffness or a gritty feel with the door wide open points at the lock.</li>
          <li><strong>Close the door and try again.</strong> If it now binds or won't throw, the lock isn't the cause.</li>
          <li><strong>Look at where the bolt lands.</strong> Watch where the bolt tip lands. If it hits the edge of the strike plate opening, or the plate shows fresh bright scratches, the bolt is landing off-center.</li>
        </ol>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Doors can swell and shift with the seasons, and hinge screws loosen with daily use. A shift you can barely see is enough to stop a bolt from sliding into its hole. If the test points at the lock itself, a <a href="/services/residential-locksmith/dover-de/lock-repair/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">lock repair in Dover</a> visit can tell you whether a part is worn or the whole lock is done.
        </p>

        <h2 id="quick-fixes-to-try-first" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Quick Fixes to Try First</h2>

        <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-[#A61E22] bg-[#F8F8F5] px-6 py-4 my-8 not-italic">
          <p class="text-gray-700 mb-0"><strong class="text-[#1A1F22]">In short:</strong> Three small fixes often help a sticky deadbolt: a puff of powdered graphite in the keyway, snug hinge screws, and a small strike plate adjustment. <a href="https://www.eufy.com/blogs/video-doorbell/door-lock-repair" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Eufy</a> advises graphite-based lubricant over oil-based products, which attract dirt. Stop if the key grinds, since forcing it can snap the key off inside the lock.</p>
        </blockquote>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Home-repair sources such as <a href="https://www.thisoldhouse.com/doors/realigning-dead-bolts-strike-plates" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">This Old House</a> walk through realigning a dead bolt and strike plate step by step.
        </p>

        <img
          src="/images/lock-repair-close-up.jpg"
          alt="Hand using a screwdriver at the latch edge of a white interior door, the kind of strike plate adjustment that can fix a sticking lock"
          width="678"
          height="452"
          class="w-full h-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200 mb-8"
          loading="lazy"
        />

        <h3 id="use-graphite-not-spray-oil" class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-8 mb-3">Use graphite, not spray oil</h3>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Puff powdered graphite into the keyway and work the key in and out a few times. Skip oil-based lubricants there. They attract dirt, and grit makes the turn worse.
        </p>

        <h3 id="tighten-the-hinge-screws" class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-8 mb-3">Tighten the hinge screws</h3>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Check each hinge screw with a screwdriver, starting at the top hinge, where the door's weight pulls hardest. A door that sags a little can drop the bolt below the opening in the frame. If a screw just spins, the wood is stripped. A longer screw can sometimes bite on a hinge, but it won't fix a worn lock.
        </p>

        <h3 id="adjust-the-strike-plate" class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-8 mb-3">Adjust the strike plate</h3>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          If the bolt hits the edge of the opening, loosen the plate, nudge it toward where the bolt lands, and retighten. Move it a hair at a time, closing the door and trying the bolt after each nudge.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Stop if the key grinds as you turn it or the knob wobbles on its screws. A key forced past that point can snap off in the cylinder, and then you're facing a key extraction.
        </p>

        <h2 id="when-repair-is-the-right-call" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">When Repair Is the Right Call</h2>

        <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-[#A61E22] bg-[#F8F8F5] px-6 py-4 my-8 not-italic">
          <p class="text-gray-700 mb-0"><strong class="text-[#1A1F22]">In short:</strong> Repair a lock when one part is worn or loose and the rest is sound: loose handle screws, a dirty cylinder, or an out-of-line strike plate. If the same fault returns after a fix, the mechanism is likely worn throughout, and replacement usually beats a third repair.</p>
        </blockquote>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Locksmiths do disagree on edge cases, as a <a href="https://www.locksmithledger.com/blog/10738417/repair-or-replace-revisited" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Locksmith Ledger column on repair versus replace</a> shows. So we keep the rule simple: fix the part, not the whole lock.
        </p>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Loose knob, handle, or trim.</strong> Check the mounting screws first.</li>
          <li><strong>A sticky cylinder with no damage.</strong> Cleaning can restore the turn.</li>
          <li><strong>A latch or strike out of line.</strong> The lock works. The fit doesn't.</li>
          <li><strong>One worn component</strong> on an otherwise good lock.</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The catch is repeat failures. If the same fault comes back a second time, treat that as a signal.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          One of our technicians told us about a sticking-deadbolt call in Dover. The homeowner had already sprayed lubricant into the keyway, and it hadn't helped. The technician found the mounting screws just spinning in their holes, because the screw holes in the door were stripped out. The old lock was worn too, so tightening the screws wasn't going to hold.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          They replaced the lock. Loose screws are a repair when the wood still grips them. When the holes are gone and the lock is worn, repair stops making sense.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          If the lock is still solid and one thing is off, our <a href="/services/lock-repair/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">lock repair service</a> is the place to start.
        </p>

        <h2 id="when-replacement-is-the-right-call" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">When Replacement Is the Right Call</h2>

        <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-[#A61E22] bg-[#F8F8F5] px-6 py-4 my-8 not-italic">
          <p class="text-gray-700 mb-0"><strong class="text-[#1A1F22]">In short:</strong> Replace a lock when the damage is inside the lock itself: cracked or bent parts, internal corrosion, a bolt that will not extend, or a key that still sticks after graphite. Age alone is not a reason. Condition and repeat failure decide it.</p>
        </blockquote>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Cracked, bent, or broken parts.</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Rust or corrosion inside the lock.</strong> Surface rust on trim may be cosmetic.</li>
          <li><strong>A key that still sticks after graphite.</strong></li>
          <li><strong>A bolt that won't extend or retract.</strong></li>
          <li><strong>A break-in.</strong> Damaged hardware shouldn't stay on the door.</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          A deadbolt with a broken tailpiece, the small bar on the back of the cylinder that turns the bolt mechanism, is a gray area. In an informal reader poll, <a href="https://www.locksmithledger.com/blog/10738417/repair-or-replace-revisited" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Locksmith Ledger</a> found that most locksmiths would replace the whole deadbolt, while a few would swap only the cylinder or tailpiece. We lean toward replacing when the lock has failed before.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Replacement can also be a security upgrade. Our <a href="/services/lock-change/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">lock change service</a> swaps a worn lock, and a <a href="/services/deadbolt-installation/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">deadbolt installation</a> adds one where there isn't a good one. Need help choosing? See our guide to the <a href="/blog/best-locks-for-your-front-door/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">best door locks for your home</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="repair-vs-replace-which-fits-your-problem" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Repair vs. Replace: Which Fits Your Problem?</h2>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Match your symptom to the likely cause, then pick the fix. Alignment trouble, loose parts, and dirty cylinders call for repair. Cracks, corrosion, and repeat failures call for replacement. If the lock works but keys are the worry, rekey.
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 not-prose">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-sm border border-gray-200">
            <thead class="bg-[#1A1F22] text-white">
              <tr><th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Symptom</th><th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Likely cause</th><th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Best fix</th><th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Why</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-gray-700">
              <tr class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Deadbolt sticks only when the door is closed</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Door or strike plate alignment</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Repair</td><td class="px-4 py-3">The lock works. The fit is off.</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Key and bolt stiff even with the door open</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Dry or dirty cylinder, or worn internals</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Repair first, replace if graphite fails</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Try the gentle fix, then judge.</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Loose knob, handle, or trim</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Loose or stripped screws</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Repair, if the lock is otherwise sound</td><td class="px-4 py-3">One small part. If the holes are stripped and the lock is worn, replace.</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Key turns but the bolt doesn't move</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Broken tailpiece or internal part</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Repair or replace</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Locksmiths differ. Past failures tip it to replace.</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Cracked, bent, or broken parts</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Physical damage</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Replace</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Damaged hardware won't hold up.</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Rust or corrosion inside the lock</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Wear from weather and time</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Replace</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Corroded internals rarely recover.</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Same problem returns after a fix</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Worn mechanism</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Replace</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Repeat failure is the signal.</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Lock works, but keys are unaccounted for</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Key control, not wear</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Rekey</td><td class="px-4 py-3">New keys, same hardware.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Use the table as a starting point. Two locks with the same symptom can need different fixes.
        </p>

        <h2 id="should-you-rekey-instead" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Should You Rekey Instead?</h2>

        <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-[#A61E22] bg-[#F8F8F5] px-6 py-4 my-8 not-italic">
          <p class="text-gray-700 mb-0"><strong class="text-[#1A1F22]">In short:</strong> Rekey instead of replacing when the lock works but key control is the concern. Rekeying changes the internal pins so old keys stop working while the hardware stays on the door. It suits a new home, a lost key, or a former tenant, but it will not repair a worn lock.</p>
        </blockquote>

        <img
          src="/images/lock-rekeying.jpg"
          alt="Locksmith rekeying a front door deadbolt at dusk, an option when lock repair isn&#x27;t needed but key control is"
          width="1536"
          height="1024"
          class="w-full h-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200 mb-8"
          loading="lazy"
        />

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Think of it this way. If your problem is "this lock won't work," you're in repair or replace territory. If it's "too many people might have a key," that's rekey territory.
        </p>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>You just moved in.</strong> See our post on <a href="/blog/moving-into-new-home-rekey-or-replace-locks/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">moving into a new home: rekey or replace</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>You lost a key.</strong> The lock is fine, but the lost key is a loose end.</li>
          <li><strong>A tenant, contractor, or ex-partner had a key.</strong> Rekeying closes that door without new hardware.</li>
        </ul>

        <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-[#A61E22] bg-[#F8F8F5] px-6 py-4 my-8 not-italic">
          <p class="text-gray-700 mb-2">“The technician was amazing. Called this morning and he was here same day to replace my knob and rekey. He was quick under 8 minutes. Thank you everything”</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mb-0">Terrance M., 5-star Google review, September 2026</p>
        </blockquote>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          For timing, see <a href="/blog/how-often-should-you-rekey-your-home/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">how often to rekey your home locks</a>. When you're ready, our <a href="/services/lock-rekeying/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">lock rekeying service</a> handles it on site.
        </p>

        <h2 id="when-should-you-call-a-locksmith-in-dover" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">When Should You Call a Locksmith in Dover?</h2>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Call a locksmith when quick fixes fail, parts are broken, or security is at stake. A key snapped off in the cylinder, a bolt that won't move, or damage after a break-in are all good reasons. A repeat failure is another good reason. We come to you and explain what needs doing and why before we start.
        </p>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>The door won't lock at all.</strong> You shouldn't leave your home unsecured overnight.</li>
          <li><strong>A key is stuck or broken inside.</strong> Pulling harder makes it worse.</li>
          <li><strong>You can't tell what's wrong.</strong> A quick look beats guessing.</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          A working lock matters because many break-ins need no force. Delaware's Statistical Analysis Center counted 301 reported burglaries in Kent County in 2024, and 149 of them were unlawful entries with no force (<a href="https://sac.delaware.gov/wp-content/uploads/sites/64/2026/01/CiD-20-24-Main-Report-final-010726.pdf" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Crime in Delaware, 2020-2024</a>). That's about half. A sticky lock is easy to stop using, and a lock nobody uses protects nothing.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          We're a bonded and insured, locally owned and operated mobile locksmith. We serve <a href="/service-areas/dover-de/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">Dover, DE</a> and nearby towns, including Camden, Felton, Magnolia, Harrington, and Frederica. Call <strong>(302) 735-0050</strong> and describe what's happening. A photo of the lock and the edge of the door helps.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Outside Dover, we also repair locks in <a href="/services/residential-locksmith/felton-de/lock-repair/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">Felton</a> and <a href="/services/residential-locksmith/milford-de/lock-repair/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">Milford</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="the-short-answer" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">The Short Answer</h2>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Start with the Door-Open Test, then try graphite, hinge screws, and a strike plate nudge. Repair a sound lock with one worn part. Replace a lock that's cracked, corroded, or failing again. Rekey when the question is who holds the keys.
        </p>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Test the door first.</strong> Open versus closed tells you where the problem lives.</li>
          <li><strong>Judge by condition.</strong> One worn part means repair. Damage or repeat failure means replace.</li>
          <li><strong>Rekey for key control.</strong> It's a third option, not a lesser one.</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          If you're still not sure, call 1st Capital Locksmith at <strong>(302) 735-0050</strong>. We'll look at the lock and the door and tell you what we'd do.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6 mt-10">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mb-2">Sources</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-700">
            <li><a href="https://www.hunker.com/12315781/how-to-fix-a-jammed-door-lock/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hunker, How to Fix a Jammed Door Lock</a></li>
