        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-12 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 not-prose">
          <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
            <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
              Should I repair or replace my door lock?
              <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Repair it when one part is worn or the door is out of line. Replace it when the lock is cracked, corroded, or has failed more than once. Run the Door-Open Test first, because a sticking lock is often a door alignment problem. If you can't tell, a locksmith can check both quickly.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
            <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
              Why is my deadbolt hard to turn?
              <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">A hard-turning deadbolt points to a dry or dirty cylinder, a misaligned door, or a worn part. Try the Door-Open Test. If it turns easily with the door open, check the strike plate and hinges. If it's stiff even then, try powdered graphite, then call a locksmith.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
            <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
              Why won't my deadbolt lock when the door is closed?
              <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The bolt isn't lining up with the hole in the frame. Home-repair guides like <a href="https://www.hunker.com/12315781/how-to-fix-a-jammed-door-lock/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hunker</a> point to a deadbolt that is out of line with the strike plate. Tighten the hinge screws, look for scuffs on the strike plate, and adjust it slightly. If the lock works with the door open, the problem is the door, not the lock.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
            <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
              Can a broken lock be fixed?
              <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Often, yes, if the damage is limited to one part and the rest of the lock is sound. A loose handle, a dirty cylinder, or a misaligned strike can all be fixed. If parts are cracked, the bolt won't engage, or the same fault keeps returning, replace it.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
            <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
              Is it better to rekey or replace a lock?
              <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">It depends on the problem. Rekey when the lock works but you want old keys to stop working, such as after a move or a lost key. Replace when the lock itself is damaged or worn out. Rekeying changes who can open the lock. It doesn't repair a failing one.</p>
          </details>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
